"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][32]={"university_physics_en:M32-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M32-L1 – Sensors, counting statistics and noise limits<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M32 – Master: Experimental Instrumentation and Research Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M16: measurement uncertainty and\nsensors; M30: quantum-measurement context · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M31&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Estimate the relative counting\nuncertainty for independent events and distinguish background noise.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A sensitive detector often counts individual photons or particles\nrather than measuring only a continuous voltage. Its uncertainty may\ninclude counting fluctuations, dark events, electronic noise, drift and\ncorrelated sources. Independent rare arrivals at a constant rate admit a\nuseful Poisson model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For a Poisson random count\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof mean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Var}(N)=\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt sufficiently large observed counts the standard deviation is\nestimated as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≃<\/mo><msqrt><mi>N<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_N\\simeq\\sqrt{N}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith negligible background, the signal-to-noise ratio relative to the\nmean is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu/\\sqrt{\\mu}=\\sqrt{\\mu}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRelative counting uncertainty therefore falls only as the inverse square\nroot of count number. Subtracting independently measured background\ncounts requires adding their properly exposure-scaled variances.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≃<\/mo><msqrt><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(ideal Poisson counts)<\/mtext><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_N=\\sqrt{\\mu},\\qquad \\mathrm{SNR}\\simeq\\sqrt{\\mu}\\quad\\text{(ideal Poisson counts)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For a mean of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>400<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=400<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndetected photons,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_N=20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the relative one-sigma fluctuation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFour times as many counts give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_N=40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbut only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.5\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrelative fluctuation. The figure compares absolute standard deviations\nwith decreasing relative fluctuations; it is not experimental data.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_213_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Count statistics: absolute standard deviation grows as count square root while relative noise falls.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Count statistics: absolute standard\ndeviation grows as count square root while relative noise\nfalls.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Relative counting precision improves only with the square root of\ndetected events.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_N/N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>900<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=900<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">By what factor must\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincrease to halve relative Poisson noise?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{N_{\\mathrm{signal}}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunderestimate uncertainty with nonzero background?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≃<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_N\\simeq30<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>30<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>900<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.33<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30/900=3.33\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">By a factor of four.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Background events and, if applicable, separate background\nsubtraction add variance.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The Poisson approximation requires independent arrivals. Dead time,\nsaturation, bunching, antibunching, drift and readout noise alter the\nstatistics. The NIST source supports sensor applications, not an\nindependent derivation of the Poisson formula.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Narrow-band signal processing can suppress off-frequency noise. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.nist.gov/pml/quantum-sensors\">NIST – Quantum Sensors<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Institutional profile: cryogenic sensors and noise reduction; topic context, not a derivation of Poisson statistics; Subject relevance to lesson: M32-L1 – Sensors, counting statistics and noise limits · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M32-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M32-L2 – Lock-in amplification and phase-sensitive detection<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M32 – Master: Experimental Instrumentation and Research Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M32-L1: noise and statistics; M7:\nharmonic oscillations · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Decompose a sinusoidal signal\ninto in-phase and quadrature components and recover amplitude and\nphase.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A weak periodic signal can be masked by broadband noise. A lock-in\namplifier exploits a known reference frequency: multiplication by a\nphase-related reference followed by low-pass filtering removes\nhigh-frequency mixer terms. Bandwidth and time constant determine the\ntrade-off between averaging time and rejection of noise.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v(t)=A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)+n(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nChoose references\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\cos(\\omega t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2\\sin(\\omega t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAveraging over many periods with a suitable low-pass filter yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=A\\cos\\phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=A\\sin\\phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the coherent part. The factor of two in the reference normalization\nmatters; instruments may report differently scaled quadratures.\nAmplitude and phase follow as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>Y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\sqrt{X^2+Y^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the quadrant-aware\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">atan2<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi=\\operatorname{atan2}(Y,X)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>Y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">atan2<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\sqrt{X^2+Y^2},\\qquad\\phi=\\operatorname{atan2}(Y,X).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=50\\,\\mathrm{\\mu V}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi=30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe quadratures are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>43.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\approx43.30\\,\\mathrm{\\mu V}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=25\\,\\mathrm{\\mu V}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe recovered values are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=50\\,\\mathrm{\\mu V}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram shows the two-phase demodulation and its quadrature\nvector.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_075_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Lock-in demodulation using orthogonal references and the X-Y quadrature vector.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Lock-in demodulation using orthogonal\nreferences and the X-Y quadrature vector.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Two orthogonal references provide amplitude and a quadrant-aware\nphase.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=3\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=4\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What angle does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">atan2<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{atan2}(1,-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyield?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arctan<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\arctan(Y/X)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninadequate to identify the phase quadrant?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=5\\,\\mathrm{mV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>135<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">135^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Because the sign of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand hence the quadrant may be lost.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Frequency drift, reference-phase error, nonstationary noise and\ninsufficient averaging violate the ideal relationships. Low-pass filters\nreduce effective noise bandwidth but can distort rapidly varying\nsignals.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Even successful demodulation depends on controlled laboratory\nconditions. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.zhinst.com/ch/en/resources/principles-of-lock-in-detection/\">Zurich Instruments – Principles of Lock-in Detection<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Two-phase demodulation and low-pass filtering; Subject relevance to lesson: M32-L2 – Lock-in amplification and phase-sensitive detection · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.thinksrs.com/downloads/pdfs/applicationnotes/Lock-In%20Basics.pdf\">Stanford Research Systems – Lock-In Amplifier Basics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · PDF topic: phase-sensitive detection and quadratures; Subject relevance to lesson: M32-L2 – Lock-in amplification and phase-sensitive detection · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M32-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M32-L3 – Vacuum systems, cryostats and thermal loads<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M32 – Master: Experimental Instrumentation and Research Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M5: thermodynamics; M32-L2:\nmeasurement chains · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate an idealized pump-down\ntime and distinguish main heat-load pathways in a cryostat.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Surface, beam and superconducting-sensor experiments often require\ntight control of residual gas and heat transport. Pumps, piping\nconductance, seals and material outgassing determine attainable\npressure. A cryostat also has radiation shields, supports and electrical\nleads, all potential heat-load paths.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For constant chamber volume\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\neffective pumping speed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand gas load\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(pressure times volume per time), the balance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V\\,\\mathrm dp/\\mathrm dt=-Sp+Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts solution reads\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(t)=Q/S+[p(0)-Q/S]e^{-St/V}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe ideal pressure time constant is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=V/S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the steady limiting pressure is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\infty=Q/S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCryostat cooling additionally faces conduction, radiation and\nresidual-gas heat transfer, which are outside this pressure-only\nequation.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(t)=p_\\infty+[p(0)-p_\\infty]e^{-t/\\tau},\\quad\\tau=V/S,\\quad p_\\infty=Q/S.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>A model chamber has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=40\\,\\mathrm L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=10\\,\\mathrm{L/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=4\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\npressure decreases from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm{Pa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.68<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10/e\\approx3.68\\,\\mathrm{Pa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin one time constant. A nonzero gas load\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=0.02\\,\\mathrm{Pa\\,L/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninstead implies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.002<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\infty=0.002\\,\\mathrm{Pa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the same model. This ideal curve is not a performance prediction for\nany particular pump.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_307_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Ideal chamber pump-down curve with time constant tau and schematic chamber-to-pump connection.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Ideal chamber pump-down curve with time\nconstant tau and schematic chamber-to-pump connection.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Gas load and effective pumping speed set the ideal pressure\nevolution and limiting pressure.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>τ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=60\\,\\mathrm L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=5\\,\\mathrm{L/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.04<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=0.04\\,\\mathrm{Pa\\,L/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=20\\,\\mathrm{L/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name two paths by which heat enters a cryostat.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=12\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.002<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\infty=0.002\\,\\mathrm{Pa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example, conduction through supports and radiation from warm\nsurfaces.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Real pumping speeds and conductances depend on pressure regime and\ngas species; outgassing and leaks may dominate at low pressures.\nCryogenic operating temperature does not follow from the pump-down\nmodel. Practical safety procedures, quench protection and materials\nselection are essential.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Controlled laboratory environments enable precision spectroscopy. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://milnepublishing.geneseo.edu/introtovacuumtech/chapter/chapter-4-rough-vacuum-regime/\">SUNY Geneseo – Introduction to Vacuum Systems<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Pump-down time: chamber volume and effective pumping speed; simplified model; Subject relevance to lesson: M32-L3 – Vacuum systems, cryostats and thermal loads · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://holmes1.mib.infn.it/nucriomib/research/cryogenic-laboratory\">Università Milano-Bicocca – Cryogenic Laboratory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Application context: cryogenic cooling and detection; Subject relevance to lesson: M32-L3 – Vacuum systems, cryostats and thermal loads · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M32-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M32-L4 – Precision spectroscopy and spectral linewidths<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M32 – Master: Experimental Instrumentation and Research Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7: optics; M11: atomic physics;\nM32-L3: laboratory conditions · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish Gaussian and\nLorentzian line shapes and determine the full width at half maximum\n(FWHM).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Spectral features have finite width. Thermal velocity distributions\nin many gases lead to Gaussian Doppler broadening, while lifetime and\ncollision processes can yield Lorentzian contributions in suitable\napproximations. Real measurements can exhibit Voigt-type mixtures\nconvolved with instrumental response.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>A Gaussian centered on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith FWHM\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay be written as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ν<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I(\\nu)=I_0\\exp[-4\\ln 2\\,((\\nu-\\nu_0)/\\Delta\\nu)^2]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHalf maximum occurs at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\nu-\\nu_0|=\\Delta\\nu/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA normalized Lorentzian with the same FWHM is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_L/I_0=[1+4((\\nu-\\nu_0)/\\Delta\\nu)^2]^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt equal FWHM the Lorentzian has heavier wings. Independent Gaussian\nbroadenings combine by adding squared FWHMs; this does not apply\ndirectly to arbitrary line shapes.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ν<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{I(\\nu)}{I_0}=\\exp\\!\\left[-4\\ln2\\left(\\frac{\\nu-\\nu_0}{\\Delta\\nu}\\right)^2\\right].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For a Gaussian line centered on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">500\\,\\mathrm{THz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\nu=2\\,\\mathrm{GHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe half-maximum points occur at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>±<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu_0\\pm1\\,\\mathrm{GHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe spectrum is a model; frequency calibration, detector linearity and\nthe instrument resolution function require separate evaluation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_267_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Comparison of normalized Gaussian and Lorentzian profiles at equal full width at half maximum.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Comparison of normalized Gaussian and\nLorentzian profiles at equal full width at half maximum.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Equal FWHM does not imply identical line shape or tail\nintensity.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At what separation from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I/I_0=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\nu=6\\,\\mathrm{MHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How do the Gaussian and Lorentzian wings differ at equal FWHM?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the combined Gaussian FWHM for widths of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm{GHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\nu-\\nu_0|=3\\,\\mathrm{MHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The Lorentzian has more slowly decaying wings.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>3<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>4<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5\\,\\mathrm{GHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The Gaussian model applies only to suitable broadening mechanisms.\nSaturation, frequency jitter, nonlinear detection and limited spectral\nresolution can bias inferred shapes. The NIST source discusses line\nprofiles; the earlier Cornell lecture index supports only the topic\nplacement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Coherent X-ray sources extend related measurement ideas to a\ndifferent energy range. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.nist.gov/pml/atomic-spectroscopy-compendium-basic-ideas-notation-data-and-formulas/atomic-spectroscopy-6\">NIST – Atomic Spectroscopy: Spectral Line Shapes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Gaussian Doppler and approximately Lorentzian line shapes; Subject relevance to lesson: M32-L4 – Precision spectroscopy and spectral linewidths · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://courses.cit.cornell.edu/ece531/Lectures/Lectures.htm\">Cornell – ECE Quantum Optics Lecture Index<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lecture index on spectra and linewidth; topic-level support only; Subject relevance to lesson: M32-L4 – Precision spectroscopy and spectral linewidths · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M32-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M32-L5 – Accelerators, undulators and X-ray free-electron lasers<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M32 – Master: Experimental Instrumentation and Research Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M10: relativity; M15: beam\nacceleration; M32-L4: spectroscopy · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Estimate the on-axis resonance\nwavelength of a planar undulator and distinguish spontaneous radiation\nfrom FEL amplification.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Relativistic electrons wiggle in the alternating magnetic field of an\nundulator and radiate. An X-ray free-electron laser additionally uses\ncollective interaction between the electron bunch and the radiation\nfield to amplify emission. Beam energy spread, bunch length and\nalignment are among the factors affecting output quality.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For a planar undulator viewed on axis\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>),\nthe approximate fundamental resonance condition is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>K<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_r=\\lambda_u(1+K^2/2)/(2\\gamma^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the magnetic period,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=E_e/(m_ec^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the electron Lorentz factor and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the dimensionless undulator strength. Quadratic dependence on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmakes short wavelengths possible even with centimeter-scale magnet\nperiods. The resonance relation alone does not predict FEL gain,\nsaturation or temporal pulse structure.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>K<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">(planar undulator, on axis)<\/mtext><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_r=\\frac{\\lambda_u}{2\\gamma^2}\\left(1+\\frac{K^2}{2}\\right)\\quad\\text{(planar undulator, on axis)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.030<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_u=0.030\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=10^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.030<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.25<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.225<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_r=0.030\\cdot1.5/(2\\cdot10^8)=2.25\\cdot10^{-10}\\,\\mathrm m=0.225\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe corresponding photon energy is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.51<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5.51\\,\\mathrm{keV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram shows an alternating magnet array and an idealized electron\ntrajectory, not the complete FEL amplification process.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_320_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Planar undulator schematic with alternating magnets, a wiggling electron trajectory and emitted X-ray wave.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Planar undulator schematic with\nalternating magnets, a wiggling electron trajectory and emitted X-ray\nwave.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Magnet period and electron energy determine the ideal resonance\nwavelength.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_u=20\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=10^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the resonance equation not by itself predict FEL gain?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Wavelength decreases by a factor of four.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_r=0.10\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Gain depends additionally on collective beam–field coupling, bunch\nproperties and losses.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The formula refers to an ideal planar undulator on axis. Observation\nangle, harmonics, energy spread and magnetic errors modify the spectrum.\nThe LCLS technical source supports the resonance relation; the SLAC\noverview provides application context.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Short optical and X-ray pulses enable time-resolved dynamics. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www6.slac.stanford.edu/research/slac-science-explained/xfels\">SLAC – XFELs: Spying on Atoms and Molecules<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Undulator principle and time-resolved X-ray applications; Subject relevance to lesson: M32-L5 – Accelerators, undulators and X-ray free-electron lasers · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6452055/\">LCLS – Development of Ultrafast Capabilities for XFELs<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Planar-undulator resonance condition with Lorentz factor and K; Subject relevance to lesson: M32-L5 – Accelerators, undulators and X-ray free-electron lasers · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M32-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M32-L6 – Pump–probe measurements and temporal resolution<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M32 – Master: Experimental Instrumentation and Research Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M32-L5: short X-ray pulses; M7: waves\nand pulses · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate effective temporal\nresolution from independent Gaussian pulse widths and timing jitter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>In a pump–probe experiment an initial pulse excites a system and a\ndelayed second pulse samples its changing state. The recorded delay\ndependence is a convolution of the physical dynamics with the instrument\nresponse. Both pulse durations and fluctuations of their relative\narrival time limit attainable time resolution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For independent Gaussian pump and probe temporal profiles and\nGaussian relative timing jitter, variances add:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{\\mathrm{eff}}^2=\\sigma_{\\mathrm{pump}}^2+\\sigma_{\\mathrm{probe}}^2+\\sigma_{\\mathrm{jitter}}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEach\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a standard deviation along the same time coordinate, not an FWHM. For\na Gaussian,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>≃<\/mo><mn>2.355<\/mn><mi>σ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{FWHM}=2\\sqrt{2\\ln2}\\,\\sigma\\simeq2.355\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWidths expressed under different conventions must not be squared and\nadded without conversion.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{\\mathrm{eff}}=\\sqrt{\\sigma_{\\mathrm{pump}}^2+\\sigma_{\\mathrm{probe}}^2+\\sigma_{\\mathrm{jitter}}^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{\\mathrm{pump}}=30\\,\\mathrm{fs}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{\\mathrm{probe}}=40\\,\\mathrm{fs}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{\\mathrm{jitter}}=20\\,\\mathrm{fs}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>2900<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>53.85<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{\\mathrm{eff}}=\\sqrt{2900}\\,\\mathrm{fs}\\approx53.85\\,\\mathrm{fs}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts Gaussian FWHM is about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>126.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">126.8\\,\\mathrm{fs}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure sketches a delay scan and the broader combined temporal\nresponse.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_226_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Pump–probe timeline with pump and probe pulses, variable delay, and broadened Gaussian instrument response.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Pump–probe timeline with pump and probe\npulses, variable delay, and broadened Gaussian instrument\nresponse.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Independent Gaussian pulse and jitter widths combine as variances\nin temporal resolution.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{\\mathrm{eff}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_1=3\\,\\mathrm{fs}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_2=4\\,\\mathrm{fs}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwithout jitter.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the FWHM of a Gaussian with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=10\\,\\mathrm{fs}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why might the measured delay trace differ from the underlying\ndynamics?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{\\mathrm{eff}}=5\\,\\mathrm{fs}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>23.55<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">23.55\\,\\mathrm{fs}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Because the observation is convolved with the pump and probe\nprofiles and timing jitter.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Quadrature addition requires independent Gaussian variables.\nCorrelated jitter, asymmetric pulses and non-Gaussian responses require\ncovariance terms or direct convolution. Temporal resolution does not\nsubstitute for calibrating the zero-delay point.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Time-resolved measurements and imaging share the task of recovering\nphysical quantities from indirect observations. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6510196/\">LCLS – Pump–Probe Experimental Methodology<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Timing jitter and delay scans as experimental methods; Subject relevance to lesson: M32-L6 – Pump–probe measurements and temporal resolution · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.mdpi.com/2673-7256/4/1/5\">MDPI – Inverse Problems in Pump–Probe Spectroscopy<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Convolution of independent Gaussian widths and instrument response; Subject relevance to lesson: M32-L6 – Pump–probe measurements and temporal resolution · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M32-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M32-L7 – Tomography, projections and inverse problems<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M32 – Master: Experimental Instrumentation and Research Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M31-L5: inverse problems; M2:\nintegration · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive the Radon projection of a\nuniform disk and explain why multiple viewing angles are needed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>In computed tomography, suitably corrected detector readings provide\nprojections representing integrals of a spatial distribution along rays.\nRecovering the distribution from its projections is an inverse problem.\nAngular coverage and data quality determine which spatial features can\nbe recovered reliably.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>The two-dimensional Radon transform of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu(x,y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒏<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒏<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>⟂<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(s)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\mu(s\\mathbf n_\\theta+t\\mathbf n_\\theta^\\perp)\\,\\mathrm dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒏<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf n_\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the projection normal. For a uniform disk of radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe ray traverses a chord of length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\sqrt{R^2-s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(s)=2\\mu_0\\sqrt{R^2-s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|s|&lt;R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand zero elsewhere. Multiple views enable filtered backprojection via\nthe Fourier slice theorem under suitable ideal conditions; noise and\nlimited angular coverage complicate inversion.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(s)=2\\mu_0\\sqrt{R^2-s^2}\\quad (|s|&lt;R),\\qquad p(s)=0\\quad (|s|\\ge R).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Consider a uniform phantom disk of radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=2\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_0=0.20\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt the center,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(0)=2\\cdot0.20\\cdot2=0.80<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(dimensionless); at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=1\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.693<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.4\\sqrt3\\approx0.693<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure shows the disk phantom alongside its curved projection\nprofile.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_295_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Uniform disk with parallel measurement rays and a semicircular Radon projection curve.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Uniform disk with parallel measurement\nrays and a semicircular Radon projection curve.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The line integral of a uniform disk is proportional to its\ntraversed chord length.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=3\\,\\mathrm{cm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_0=0.1\\,\\mathrm{cm}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noutside a uniform disk\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|s|&gt;R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why do missing projection angles introduce artifacts?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(0)=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(s)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Insufficient directional Fourier sampling can render reconstruction\nunstable or nonunique.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The chord formula applies only to a uniform disk with ideal straight\nrays. Scatter, beam hardening, finite pixels, motion and limited-angle\nsampling complicate real CT. In X-ray CT the projection quantity is\ntypically obtained from transmission readings after a logarithmic\ntransformation and calibration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Good measurement data require careful experimental design as well as\nreconstruction. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.math.uni-hamburg.de/en/forschung/bereiche/am/methoden-bildgebung/lehre/computer-tomography-ws2122.html\">Universität Hamburg – Computer Tomography<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Course outline: Radon transform and inverse reconstruction; Subject relevance to lesson: M32-L7 – Tomography, projections and inverse problems · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://tristanvanleeuwen.github.io/IP_and_Im_Lectures/tomography.html\">Inverse Problems and Imaging – Computed Tomography<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Radon transform, Fourier slice theorem and filtered backprojection; Subject relevance to lesson: M32-L7 – Tomography, projections and inverse problems · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M32-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M32-L8 – Experimental design, randomization and factor effects<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M32 – Master: Experimental Instrumentation and Research Methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M16: measurement uncertainty;\nM32-L1–L7: instruments and data methods · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M32&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compute main-effect and\ninteraction coefficients in a coded ±1 full two-factor experiment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Experimental design specifies factors, levels, replicates and\nmeasurement order. Varying only one parameter at a time can miss\ninteractions. A full\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfactorial study tests all four combinations of two two-level factors;\nrandomization guards against ordering effects, while replication helps\nestimate residual variation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>At coded levels\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_A,x_B\\in\\{-1,+1\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\na simple model is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=\\beta_0+\\beta_Ax_A+\\beta_Bx_B+\\beta_{AB}x_Ax_B+\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a balanced unreplicated\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntable, coefficients follow from four readings:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_A=\\tfrac14\\sum_i x_{A,i}y_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand similarly for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">AB<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the grand mean. With ±1 coding the high-minus-low main effect is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\beta_A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOne observation per cell leaves no independent residual degrees of\nfreedom for pure-error estimation.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>4<\/mn><\/munderover><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Effect<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_A=\\frac14\\sum_{i=1}^{4}x_{A,i}y_i,\\qquad\\operatorname{Effect}(A)=2\\beta_A.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Consider fabricated\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nresponses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_{--}=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_{+-}=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_{-+}=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>18<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_{++}=18<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe grand mean is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_0=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>18<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_A=(12+18-8-10)/4=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_B=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_{AB}=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe observed main effects are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>6<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>4<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nresponse units; the interaction coefficient is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncorresponding to a two-unit interaction contrast under this convention.\nThe diagram displays all four treatment combinations.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_089_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two-by-two factorial matrix at coded A and B levels with fabricated responses 8, 12, 10 and 18.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two-by-two factorial matrix at coded A\nand B levels with fabricated responses 8, 12, 10 and 18.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>A full two-factor design enables calculation of main effects and\ninteractions.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the A main effect for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_A=2.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunder ±1 coding.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why are repeated readings at each factor combination valuable?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=\\beta_0+\\beta_Ax_A+\\beta_Bx_B+\\beta_{AB}x_Ax_B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhich coefficient encodes the interaction of A and B?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\beta_A=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Replication allows independent noise estimates and reproducibility\nchecks.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_{AB}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The four treatment combinations describe effects within the selected\nfactor ranges. With no replication, the saturated model cannot\nindependently estimate pure experimental error. Randomization, blocking,\ncalibration, safety controls and causal interpretation still require\nexperimental justification. NIST supports the design framework; the\nstatistical course clarifies the coefficient convention.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The module connects counting statistics, signal processing, research\ninfrastructure, reconstruction and reproducible experimentation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pri/section3/pri3.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH – Choosing an Experimental Design<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Objectives and randomized/factorial design choices; overview only; Subject relevance to lesson: M32-L8 – Experimental design, randomization and factor effects · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat503/Lesson06\">Penn State – STAT 503: The 2^k Factorial Design<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Coded factors and distinction between main effect and regression coefficient; Subject relevance to lesson: M32-L8 – Experimental design, randomization and factor effects · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
